426	28	2	B	Aymoon and Books	https://codeforces.com/group/3nQaj5GMG5/contest/374321/problem/B	{"meta": {"title": "B. Aymoon and Books", "inputType": "standard input", "timeLimit": "1 second", "outputType": "standard output", "memoryLimit": "256 megabytes", "timeLimitMs": 1000, "memoryLimitMB": 256}, "note": null, "type": "problem", "story": "<div><p>Aymoon has a very nice bookshelf containing <span class=\\"tex-span\\"><i>N</i></span> books in a row. Each book has a unique id. He can only pick either the leftmost or rightmost book. Aymoon will keep picking books from the shelf in some order and put them on a table and he can only read any book that is on the table. However, some books are more interesting than others. Aymoon believes that the book with the greatest id is the most interesting book to read.</p><p>You are given the description of Aymoon's bookshelf and some queries. <span class=\\"tex-font-style-bf\\">Each query will depend on all the previous queries</span>. Each query will be one of three types:</p><ul> <li> '<span class=\\"tex-font-style-tt\\">L</span>': Remove the leftmost book and put it on the table (if there are no books on the shelf, do nothing). </li><li> '<span class=\\"tex-font-style-tt\\">R</span>': Remove the rightmost book and put it on the table (if there are no books on the shelf, do nothing). </li><li> '<span class=\\"tex-font-style-tt\\">Q</span>': Tell Aymoon the id of the most interesting book that is currently on the table to read it. </li></ul><p>Note that if Aymoon read a book, he will put it away from the table and will never read it again.</p></div>", "inputSpec": "<p>The first line contains a single integer <span class=\\"tex-span\\"><i>N</i></span> (<span class=\\"tex-span\\">1</span> <span class=\\"tex-span\\">≤</span> <span class=\\"tex-span\\"><i>N</i></span> <span class=\\"tex-span\\">≤</span> <span class=\\"tex-span\\">10<sup class=\\"upper-index\\">5</sup></span>) — the number of books on the shelf.</p><p>The second line contains <span class=\\"tex-span\\"><i>N</i></span> distinct integers <span class=\\"tex-span\\"><i>a</i><sub class=\\"lower-index\\">1</sub>, <i>a</i><sub class=\\"lower-index\\">2</sub>, ..., <i>a</i><sub class=\\"lower-index\\"><i>N</i></sub></span> (<span class=\\"tex-span\\">1 ≤ <i>a</i><sub class=\\"lower-index\\"><i>i</i></sub> ≤ 10<sup class=\\"upper-index\\">5</sup></span>) — the ids of the books on the shelf from left to right.</p><p>The third line contains a single integer <span class=\\"tex-span\\"><i>Q</i></span> (<span class=\\"tex-span\\">1 ≤ <i>Q</i> ≤ 10<sup class=\\"upper-index\\">5</sup></span>) — the number of queries. </p><p>Then <span class=\\"tex-span\\"><i>Q</i></span> lines follow each of them contains a single character representing the query type '<span class=\\"tex-font-style-tt\\">L</span>', '<span class=\\"tex-font-style-tt\\">R</span>', or '<span class=\\"tex-font-style-tt\\">Q</span>'.</p>", "testCases": [{"id": 1, "input": "5\\n6 1 7 5 4\\n5\\nL\\nQ\\nR\\nL\\nQ", "output": "6\\n4"}], "outputSpec": "<p>For each query of type '<span class=\\"tex-font-style-tt\\">Q</span>', print the id of the most interesting book on the table. If there are no books on the table at the moment the query is done, print <span class=\\"tex-span\\"> - 1</span>.</p><p>If there are no queries of type '<span class=\\"tex-font-style-tt\\">Q</span>', <span class=\\"tex-font-style-bf\\">don't output anything</span>.</p>"}	\N	\N	374321
