508	19	5	E	Shape	https://codeforces.com/group/E23MsQv91X/contest/313310/problem/E	{"meta": {"title": "E. Shape", "inputType": "standard input", "timeLimit": "1 second", "outputType": "standard output", "memoryLimit": "256 megabytes", "timeLimitMs": 1000, "memoryLimitMB": 256}, "note": null, "type": "problem", "story": "<div><p>Given an odd integer <span class=\\"cf-inline-math\\">\\\\(N\\\\)</span>, for every <span class=\\"cf-inline-math\\">\\\\(N\\\\)</span> print the following shape: (<span class=\\"cf-inline-math\\">\\\\(N\\\\)</span> is always odd)</p><p>Examples:</p><pre class=\\"lstlisting\\"><code class=\\"prettyprint prettyprinted\\" style=\\"\\"><span class=\\"kwd\\">for</span><span class=\\"pln\\"> N </span><span class=\\"pun\\">=</span><span class=\\"pln\\"> </span><span class=\\"lit\\">3</span><span class=\\"pln\\"> </span><span class=\\"kwd\\">print</span><span class=\\"pun\\">:</span><br><br><span class=\\"pun\\">*</span><span class=\\"pln\\"> </span><span class=\\"pun\\">*</span><br><span class=\\"pun\\">***</span><br><span class=\\"pun\\">*</span><span class=\\"pln\\"> </span><span class=\\"pun\\">*</span><br><br><span class=\\"kwd\\">for</span><span class=\\"pln\\"> N </span><span class=\\"pun\\">=</span><span class=\\"pln\\"> </span><span class=\\"lit\\">5</span><span class=\\"pln\\"> </span><span class=\\"kwd\\">print</span><span class=\\"pun\\">:</span><br><br><span class=\\"pun\\">*</span><span class=\\"pln\\">   </span><span class=\\"pun\\">*</span><br><span class=\\"pun\\">**</span><span class=\\"pln\\"> </span><span class=\\"pun\\">**</span><br><span class=\\"pun\\">*****</span><br><span class=\\"pun\\">**</span><span class=\\"pln\\"> </span><span class=\\"pun\\">**</span><br><span class=\\"pun\\">*</span><span class=\\"pln\\">   </span><span class=\\"pun\\">*</span><br><br><span class=\\"kwd\\">for</span><span class=\\"pln\\"> N </span><span class=\\"pun\\">=</span><span class=\\"pln\\"> </span><span class=\\"lit\\">7</span><span class=\\"pln\\"> </span><span class=\\"kwd\\">print</span><span class=\\"pun\\">:</span><br><br><span class=\\"pun\\">*</span><span class=\\"pln\\">     </span><span class=\\"pun\\">*</span><br><span class=\\"pun\\">**</span><span class=\\"pln\\">   </span><span class=\\"pun\\">**</span><br><span class=\\"pun\\">***</span><span class=\\"pln\\"> </span><span class=\\"pun\\">***</span><br><span class=\\"pun\\">*******</span><br><span class=\\"pun\\">***</span><span class=\\"pln\\"> </span><span class=\\"pun\\">***</span><br><span class=\\"pun\\">**</span><span class=\\"pln\\">   </span><span class=\\"pun\\">**</span><br><span class=\\"pun\\">*</span><span class=\\"pln\\">     </span><span class=\\"pun\\">*</span><br></code></pre></div>", "inputSpec": "<p>Only one line containing a number <span class=\\"cf-inline-math\\">\\\\(N\\\\)</span> (<span class=\\"cf-inline-math\\">\\\\( 1 \\\\le N \\\\le 50 \\\\)</span>).</p>", "testCases": [{"id": 1, "input": "3", "output": "* *\\n***\\n* *"}, {"id": 2, "input": "5", "output": "*   *\\n** **\\n*****\\n** **\\n*   *"}], "outputSpec": "<p>print <span class=\\"cf-inline-math\\">\\\\(N\\\\)</span> lines each contains <span class=\\"cf-inline-math\\">\\\\(N\\\\)</span> characters</p>"}	\N	\N	313310
509	19	7	G	Palindrome again	https://codeforces.com/group/E23MsQv91X/contest/313310/problem/G	{"meta": {"title": "G. Palindrome again", "inputType": "standard input", "timeLimit": "2 s.", "outputType": "standard output", "memoryLimit": "256 MB", "timeLimitMs": 2000, "memoryLimitMB": 256}, "note": null, "type": "problem", "story": "<div><p>A string is called palindrome if it reads the same from left to right and from right to left. For example \\"<span class=\\"tex-font-style-tt\\">aba</span>\\", \\"<span class=\\"tex-font-style-tt\\">oo</span>\\", \\"<span class=\\"tex-font-style-tt\\">r</span>\\" and \\" are palindroms, but strings \\"<span class=\\"tex-font-style-tt\\">mahmoud</span>\\" and \\"<span class=\\"tex-font-style-tt\\">ashour</span>\\" are not.</p><p>You are given string <span class=\\"cf-inline-math\\">\\\\(s\\\\)</span> consisting of lowercase English letters. with one move you can choose any index in the string and change letter in that index to any other lowercase letter. You can change the order of the string <span class=\\"cf-inline-math\\">\\\\(s\\\\)</span> as you want . <span class=\\"tex-font-style-bf\\">changing the order doesn't count as moves.</span> </p><p>You should obtain palindrome with the minimal number of moves. If there are several ways to do that you should get the lexicographically smallest one.</p></div>", "inputSpec": "<p>The only line contains string <span class=\\"cf-inline-math\\">\\\\(s\\\\)</span> (<span class=\\"cf-inline-math\\">\\\\(1 \\\\le |s| \\\\le 2 \\\\cdot 10^5\\\\)</span>) consisting of only lowercase English letters.</p>", "testCases": [{"id": 1, "input": "aabc", "output": "abba"}, {"id": 2, "input": "aabcd", "output": "abcba"}], "outputSpec": "<p>Print the lexicographically smallest palindrome that can be obtained with the minimal number of moves.</p>"}	\N	\N	313310
510	10	26	Z	Triangle	https://codeforces.com/group/MWSDmqGsZm/contest/223340/problem/Z	{"meta": {"title": "Z. Triangle", "inputType": "stdin", "timeLimit": "2 seconds", "outputType": "stdout", "memoryLimit": "64 megabytes", "timeLimitMs": 2000, "memoryLimitMB": 64}, "note": null, "type": "problem", "story": "<div><p>At a geometry lesson Bob learnt that a triangle is called right-angled if it is nondegenerate and one of its angles is right. Bob decided to draw such a triangle immediately: on a sheet of paper he drew three points with integer coordinates, and joined them with segments of straight lines, then he showed the triangle to Peter. Peter said that Bob's triangle is not right-angled, but is <span class=\\"tex-font-style-underline\\">almost</span> right-angled: the triangle itself is not right-angled, but it is possible to move one of the points exactly by distance 1 so, that all the coordinates remain integer, and the triangle become right-angled. Bob asks you to help him and find out if Peter tricks him. By the given coordinates of the triangle you should find out if it is right-angled, almost right-angled, or neither of these.</p></div>", "inputSpec": "<p>The first input line contains 6 space-separated integers <span class=\\"tex-span\\"><i>x</i><sub class=\\"lower-index\\">1</sub>, <i>y</i><sub class=\\"lower-index\\">1</sub>, <i>x</i><sub class=\\"lower-index\\">2</sub>, <i>y</i><sub class=\\"lower-index\\">2</sub>, <i>x</i><sub class=\\"lower-index\\">3</sub>, <i>y</i><sub class=\\"lower-index\\">3</sub></span> — coordinates of the triangle's vertices. All the coordinates are integer and don't exceed 100 in absolute value. It's guaranteed that the triangle is nondegenerate, i.e. its total area is not zero.</p>", "testCases": [{"id": 1, "input": "0 0 2 0 0 1", "output": "RIGHT"}, {"id": 2, "input": "2 3 4 5 6 6", "output": "NEITHER"}, {"id": 3, "input": "-1 0 2 0 0 1", "output": "ALMOST"}], "outputSpec": "<p>If the given triangle is right-angled, output <span class=\\"tex-font-style-tt\\">RIGHT</span>, if it is almost right-angled, output <span class=\\"tex-font-style-tt\\">ALMOST</span>, and if it is neither of these, output <span class=\\"tex-font-style-tt\\">NEITHER</span>.</p>"}	\N	900	223340
